<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <!-- Entitlements for a DMG people download and run, signed with a Developer
       ID. This is NOT the App Store build: see entitlements.plist for that.

       The difference that matters is the App Sandbox, which is absent here.
       Outside the App Store it is optional, and switching it on would silently
       break two things AegisDraft actually does:

         - the wigolo sidecar, which is launched through `npx` (docs/SIDECAR.md)
         - PDF sources, which shell out to `pdftotext` (docs/KNOWLEDGE-OS.md)

       A sandboxed direct-download build would install cleanly and then fail at
       both, with no error a person could act on. Shipping the sandbox here
       would buy nothing and cost those features.

       Everything the sandbox was protecting still holds: the app reads and
       writes the project folder the author picked, talks to Ollama on
       loopback, and reaches the network only through the two gated paths in
       the golden rule (docs/ARCHITECTURE.md#golden-rule). -->

  <!-- Hardened Runtime is required for notarization. -->
  <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory</key>
  <false/>

  <!-- Ollama on 127.0.0.1:11434, and wigolo on 127.0.0.1:3333. -->
  <key>com.apple.security.network.client</key>
  <true/>

  <!-- The app listens on nothing. -->
  <key>com.apple.security.network.server</key>
  <false/>

  <!-- Deliberately absent: com.apple.security.app-sandbox. -->
</dict>
</plist>
